# runs only in 32 and 64 bits: its numbers take more room than a 16-bit program has
# crypto.bignum against Python's own big numbers: Montgomery's product (a b / R mod n) and powers
# (a^e mod n), sums and differences (a + b and a - b mod n) for moduli of every size from 1 byte to 256, with the sizes that fill a 32-bit part
# exactly, fall one byte short and go one byte over; moduli that are all ones or just past a power
# of two, with numbers next to them, so that every carry there can be happens; and the primes and
# orders of P-256 and P-384. Each case's numbers are hex, as wide as the modulus.
